      Created &lt;time title=&quot; 3 June 2025 at 18:41&quot; datetime=&quot;2025-06-03T18:41:59Z&quot;&gt;about 1 year ago&lt;/time&gt; by &lt;a class=&quot;mw-100 d-inline-block align-bottom text-truncate text-wrap&quot; dir=&quot;auto&quot; href=&quot;https://www.openstreetmap.org/user/Ian%20Pyle&quot;&gt;Ian Pyle&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;note-comment-text&quot;&gt;Old railway lines through Kisallomas, replace by new lines through Tocovolgy / 
      Resolved &lt;time title=&quot;18 August 2026 at 22:02&quot; datetime=&quot;2026-08-18T22:02:34Z&quot;&gt;about 5 hours ago&lt;/time&gt; by &lt;a class=&quot;mw-100 d-inline-block align-bottom text-truncate text-wrap&quot; dir=&quot;auto&quot; href=&quot;https://www.openstreetmap.org/user/&amp;#39;Lex&quot;&gt;&amp;#39;Lex&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;note-comment-text&quot;&gt;Yes, there used to be an old Kisállomás station around here. You can see pictures of it here: https://debrecenikepeslapok.blogspot.com/2026/04/a-vasarteri-kisallomas-18841991.html
It was demolished 20+ years ago though, so nothing remains. The Kisállomás was &amp;#39;moved&amp;#39;; the new station is Tócósvölgy, as you mentioned: https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/549344791

There is a blog post in which someone tries to showcase the old tracks (108 and 109) on old aerial images and identify any traces of them that are still visible on the ground: https://nevtelenutazo.blog.hu/2023/02/24/2022_11_05_a_109-es_es_108-as_regi_nyomvonala
Nowadays, nothing remains that could be mapped.</title>
